            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to do with energy, then the Latin for to tire out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: causidicus, causidicī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "causidicus, causidicī — \"an advocate, a pleader for hire\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Causidicus prō reō dīxit. The advocate spoke for the accused."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "causidicus, causidicī m., from causa, a case, and dīcō: a case-speaker, often said with a little scorn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "causidicus is built from causa \"a case at law\" and dīcō \"to say\" (Lewis & Short): one who speaks cases."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
